\subsection{Why both \code{ensure_all()} and \code{ensure_none()}}{

The two are not each other's negation, because they treat a missing
value differently. A row violates \code{ensure_all()} unless the condition is
\code{TRUE}, so a row where it is \code{NA} is a violation: the rule cannot be shown
to hold. In \code{ensure_none()} such a row passes, as it would in
\code{\link[dplyr:filter]{dplyr::filter()}}.

